WEBVTT

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.480
Winter in Gloucestershire.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.800
Trees stand bare and sentinel, but spring is just around the corner, bringing with it new life.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.400
It's January, the time for out with the old and in with the new.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.920
And here at Batsford Arboretum it's no exception.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.960
With the help of this sonic hammer, they can give their trees a health check and detect any sign of decay.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.720
Ellie's flying high.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:18.400
Here in the middle of the Gloucestershire countryside, you wouldn't expect to see vultures, but this place is the world authority when it comes to birds of prey.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:27.640
Tom's looking at a new law which has been introduced to tackle the crisis of neglected and abandoned horses, but not everybody's happy about the situation.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.160
What were you going to say, sir?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.280
OK, for grazing, you mean?

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:42.640
And Adam's making a return visit to one of the most inspirational young farmers he's ever met.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.520
This year's Food & Farming Awards are upon us again.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:56.480
I've travelled to Scotland to catch up with one of last year's finalists, Cameron Hendry, and to get the search for Countryfile's 2016 Farming Hero underway.

00:02:09.199 --> 00:02:14.560
The green, green grass of Gloucestershire.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:21.480
Be it crops or flocks, the rich earth here makes it the perfect county for growing things.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:29.520
And with Adam's farm and Ellie's apple orchard both in Gloucestershire, it looks like my fellow Countryfile presenters agree.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.840
But they're not the only ones to have put down roots here.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.760
Trees seem to triumph in this Gloucestershire soil and I've come to a place that is full of them.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.320
Big and small.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:52.480
Batsford Arboretum, near Moreton-in-Marsh, has been collecting and caring for exotic tree species since the mid-19th century.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.800
Head gardener Matthew Hall has been meeting the needs of the trees here for more than 12 years.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.280
Yeah, really good after a fantastic walk.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:01.640
What a place.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.400
Fantastic place, isn't it?

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:02.680
Yeah.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.640
What's this, for example?

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.760
Shishigashira, wonderful tree.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.320
Almost like big walking sticks, aren't they?

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.320
Beautiful.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.680
Do you know how many trees you've got here?

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.680
We've got 3,000 trees.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.440
Right.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.920
In that lot, we would have about 1,600 individual, different trees, different species of tree.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.240
Some of them are obviously incredibly old, so when did all this start and whose idea was it?

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.120
It goes back to about the 1870s, 1880s.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:44.040
The arboretum was the brainchild of Victorian eccentric Lord Redesdale after returning from diplomatic posts in Russia, China and Japan.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:53.640
His love of the Orient inspired him to transform Batsford, tearing out formal beds in favour of wild planting and exotic trees.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:59.560
Later owners expanded the collection, but Redesdale's trees formed the backbone of the arboretum.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.760
Are you now constantly evolving this place?

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.840
Is it going to get bigger?

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.000
It's always evolving.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.080
We've added about another 15 acres.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.640
We're planting about 70 or 80 plants a year.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.280
We can't be a museum, we've got to move forward.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.760
Trees come out, new ones go in, so it's always evolving.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:21.839
Among the dazzling array of species at Batsford is an old tree which is now in a bad way.

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:27.760
Right, then, Matt, we've got this purple beech here we've got a few problems with.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.480
You've obviously been working on it already.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.360
What's the situation with it?

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:33.120
Well, we've got bracket fungi on the graft line.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.880
You just see around there, a ganoderma.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.680
Right.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.080
Unfortunately, we've got an area of decay around the front.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.440
Quite a lot of science going on in here as well.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.840
We like a bit of science.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.080
Got all these sensors on.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.680
Let's go have a look, see what's happening.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:58.040
A team from Oxford University is using new technology that allows them to look inside the trunk to see how bad the damage is, like an X-ray for trees.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:59.760
Ian Sherwood is the man with the scanner.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.680
Right, then, Ian, this is all looking incredibly technical.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.720
What's happening here?

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.600
We're just doing a tomograph survey of the tree.

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:18.000
That sends sound waves through the tree and it gives you a reading of what's going on inside the tree without actually drilling in.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.480
The sensors are placed in a ring around the trunk.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.640
Then each one is tapped in turn with Ian's sonic hammer.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:30.600
Very Doctor Who!

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.680
He's got a sonic screwdriver, so between us, we can fix the TARDIS.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:44.600
And no sci-fi set-up would be complete without a slightly stroppy computer.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.840
'Please tap again.'

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:47.640
'Please tap again.'

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:50.080
'Measurements at this spot have been recorded.'

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.120
Time to see just how rotten our tree is.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.720
Right, so very colourful.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:00.200
Yeah, it is very colourful.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.080
Blue, that could be a cavity, or certainly very decayed wood.

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:14.640
The pink is still severe decay, but not quite so much, so you can see the progression back through the trunk and it's actually quite extensive.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.440
Yeah.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:19.200
And, Matthew, this sad reality confirms what you were thinking.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.360
This is quite sad, really, but, yeah, unfortunately, it'll have to go.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:42.720
Well, taking a tree down of this size is a specialist skill and later on, I'm going to be seeing how they do it, but first, right now across the country, there are thousands of neglected and abandoned horses facing a winter outdoors.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:48.600
It's a problem that's reached crisis levels over the last few years, but as Tom's been finding out, a new law could bring an end to that suffering.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:57.280
A winter's morning in Yorkshire.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:02.080
I'm on the road with Stockton-on-Tees council animal welfare inspector Steve Gale.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:10.360
His wide-ranging experience means other councils come to him for advice on illegal grazing.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:10.880
There's a few just here.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:11.160
Four there.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:16.160
And sure enough, among the parked cars and playgrounds, horses, more than a dozen of them.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:21.720
And they're not tethered, are they?

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.240
Don't think so.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:25.040
Those three, I don't think any of those could be tethered.

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:35.120
When owners turn their horses onto someone else's land for a free meal, it's called fly-grazing.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.080
But that's only half the problem.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:46.000
It's also common for unwanted horses to be simply abandoned and left on roadsides, fields or housing estates.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:46.280
We're taking a closer look.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:49.560
One of the horses is tethered by a rope pegged into the ground.

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:55.080
It's worn a clear circle, bare of any grass.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.440
Is this a typical fly-grazing scene?

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:58.200
When we talk about fly-grazing, is this what we mean?

00:07:58.320 --> 00:07:59.560
This is what we mean.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:07.400
Probably illegally grazed on, I suspect, council land, so it hasn't got the landowner's approval to be here.

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:15.320
We've got two loose horses which obviously in themselves can cause problems, cos they are running around round the estate.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.880
And as you can see from the ground, the fact that it's tethered in one place, it's got no grazing.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.680
It's not really got any water.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:27.840
MAN SHOUTS Steve's visits are not always welcome and as locals appear to check on their horses and us, our police escort steps in.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.000
What were you going to say, sir?

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:32.760
The council should...?

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:37.000
OK, for grazing, you mean?

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:42.760
Hopefully, the mallet's just for the horse's tether.

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:43.919
What do you make of that?

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:46.360
Typical response, to be honest.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:53.240
When a farmer wants some livestock, he buys the farm and then he buys the animals, but these guys tend to seem to do it differently.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:59.320
They get the animals, then they're not sure where to graze them, then they think it's our duty to provide them with some grazing.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.400
So why are these horses here?

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:05.360
As other owners arrive, one local resident, Ian Gregory, is keen to explain.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.880
Why do you keep a horse as a pet?

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.960
Cos I might think I'll keep a cat or a dog, but a horse?

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:12.600
That's a big undertaking.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:18.320
Well, for me, it's a hobby.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.480
Do you know what I mean?

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.120
I like it.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:23.280
It keeps me out all the time.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:23.920
Mm-hm.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.600
It gets me to do stuff where, like, a cat, it's different, innit, you know what I mean?

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:28.720
Yeah.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:28.880
But, like...

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.640
And you think you can do the job responsibly of keeping a horse, do you?

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.120
Oh, yeah, I know I can.

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:35.440
I've brought that one up.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:38.120
But do you actually own any pasture of your own?

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:39.720
Oh, no, no, no, no.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:44.480
Do you think it's right to be just borrowing or just going on to a bit of land and finding a bit of grazing?

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:44.920
Do you think that's right?

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:48.800
Truthful, I don't see the harm in it.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:57.520
While Ian's pony Billy Boy seems in good health, the RSPCA say fly-grazing is often linked to poor welfare.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:00.920
They say it's a crisis involving thousands of horses.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:13.960
But that's starting to change - first in Wales, then last summer in England, control of horses laws were introduced which should mean happier outcomes for neglected animals.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:22.240
The new legislation allows any landowner in England and councils in Wales who find a horse on their property to seize it.

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:28.440
If it's not claimed, they can sell the animal or give it to charity.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.560
Some of this work is being done by the RSPCA.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:34.800
Increasingly, landowners are calling in equine bailiffs to do the job.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.240
Spread out.

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:43.040
Concentrate on the left-hand side.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:49.880
One company specialising in this area is Bristol-based GRC Bailiffs.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:55.360
They travel the entire country seizing illegally grazed horses, usually in the dead of night.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:11:02.440
These are military-style operations involving horse experts and security personnel.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:05.320
Under the new law, seizing horses is relatively simple.

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:14.440
The legislation is a lot better now, because it clarifies what landowners can do and how they can go about it.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:19.800
In England, the bailiffs don't have to give notice.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:27.440
They just round up the horses and if the owner wants them back, they'll have to foot the bill for the whole operation.

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:28.600
It's hardly surprising this causes some ill feeling.

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:33.960
The team have asked us to disguise their identities in case of repercussions.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.000
The owners may turn up and try and interfere with the operation.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:39.800
If someone starts screaming and shouting, we'll never get the horses loaded.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:47.200
We'd just rather come out early morning, later in the evening, and get it done then without being interfered with.

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:51.960
It's a vanishing act that's not appreciated by the owners.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:57.640
Have you ever had your horses seized by the council?

00:11:56.840 --> 00:12:07.720
And what did you think about that?

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:19.640
He may not be happy, but supporters say it is an effective and fast way to remove horses.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:31.720
It sounds perfect - a new power to seize horses and deal with any welfare concerns, but there's a fly in the ointment.

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:35.080
What happens to all those rescued horses?

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.040
Well, the truth is, they don't all live a life in clover.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.480
Indeed, some could end up being destroyed and I'll be looking into that later.

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:57.240
Even in the depths of winter, the beauty of the natural world is a sight to behold.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:07.400
Light reflects off ice, freezing mists enfold the land and the low sun glints through silent woodlands.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:16.400
These endless wonders of nature have inspired and captured the imagination of artists for thousands of years.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:21.400
Everything from beasts to bees, wild woods to weather.

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:31.320
Nature In Art is the name of the world's first gallery dedicated exclusively to art that depicts nature.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:38.080
Based here at Wallsworth Hall near Gloucester, there's art and sculptures from all over the world.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:41.120
Simon, hello, good to meet you.

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:43.240
'Simon Trapnell is the director of the museum.'

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.120
Tell me a bit about the gallery.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.920
When did it first start?

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:54.760
We opened in 1988 and the idea was born in 1982, that's when we had the dream.

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:56.760
Where did the dream come from?

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:58.640
I think the dream was the result, actually, of my mum.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:13:59.600
I have to blame her.

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:19.760
She's an artist, always been inspired by nature, and my father realised that most genres of art seemed to have a natural home where you could go and celebrate a particular style or school of work, but rather unbelievably, there didn't seem to be anywhere that focused exclusively on art inspired by nature.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:22.680
He resolved, "Maybe we ought to try and plug that gap," and Nature In Art is the plug.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:25.920
Is there a particular philosophy that you live by today?

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:34.960
We want to celebrate what people expect - maybe a Peter Scott or a David Shepherd or whatever.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:39.960
I think we need to be a place too that gives people surprises and they see things they don't expect.

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:48.720
And there's lots to see, surprises at every turn, art of all descriptions inspired by nature.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.680
But for one young artist, the gallery itself is an inspiration.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.160
Hi, Holly.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.440
Hi, Ellie.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:55.320
What are you looking at here?

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:58.560
I think this is one of my favourite pieces in the whole gallery.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.440
Why is it your favourite?

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:01.560
Well, I...

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:12.480
'Regular visits to this gallery 'to see works like this woodcut by George Tute 'has had a profound effect on 21-year-old artist Holly Brookes.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:16.800
'But it's the world beyond the walls where she truly finds inspiration.'

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:21.440
What kind of thing do you normally look for?

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:25.640
Well, anything that really fires up my imagination, really.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:28.760
I was here the other day and I saw these beautiful berries down here.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:28.880
Yeah!

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.840
It's lovely to see really bright colours with the sludgy winter palette, isn't it?

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.680
Definitely.

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:35.720
It kind of livens up the landscape.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.080
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:38.480
But I think today I'm looking for something a bit wilder.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.200
Oh, wow.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:54.240
These are beautiful in their natural forms here.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:56.640
These burs?

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:59.960
I wouldn't have even seen beauty here, but now with looking at them in this light...

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:06.080
I think it's this lovely contrast we're getting of the very pale tones and then this strong shadow coming in on the side here.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:11.600
But would you create your art out here, or try and take some of this back with you inside?

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:14.040
Well, I think what I would do first off is take a few quick shots.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.800
Will you take a sketch as well while you're here?

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:20.640
Yes.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:26.760
As long as I put down some marks on the page, that will really help to bring this back to life for me when I look at them later on, I find that quite useful.

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:32.840
Yeah.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:41.960
It was time spent by the sea that gave Holly the subject matter for this picture, a razorbill caught in netting.

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:45.320
It won her a top prize at an international wildlife art competition.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:53.520
For your prize-winning piece of art, what was the inspiration behind that?

00:16:52.840 --> 00:17:16.400
I was studying in Aberystwyth at the time and I went out walking after a series of very strong storms and I just couldn't help noticing all these dead bird carcasses washed up and there was this one in particular that was all tangled up in this blue netting and so there were these very vibrant colours and I really thought that I could maybe emulate some of the old Dutch still-life masters and that's really what I was going for with this, the contrast between life, death, beauty, brutality, with a kind of conservation message underlying it all.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:22.119
Holly's meticulous work out in the field is just the start.

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:34.640
It takes hours and hours of intense effort to turn her ideas into prints and drawings.

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:42.160
Why do you use nature as your subject matter?

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:48.640
As an environment to be working in, it's quite dynamic, almost like a theatre production, really.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:51.160
You've got either end of the emotional spectrum...

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:51.760
Yeah.

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:52.200
..Played out before you in nature.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:18:00.640
For me, with my love of detail and texture, there's just endless variety in the natural world to work with, so I don't know, really.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:03.040
Ultimately, I guess, it just captures my imagination.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:16.480
These are absolutely extraordinary and so intricate.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:26.120
Because I love the natural world, artists who manage to capture a moment of nature frozen in time makes me appreciate it all the more.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:37.920
Now, here's our weekly winter warmer to beat the season's chill.

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:43.200
Last summer, we asked some well-known faces, from athletes to comedians...

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:45.200
Oh, it's quite refreshing after a while.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:48.920
..Actresses to chefs...

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.280
Bon appetit.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:55.200
..What part of our magnificent countryside was special to them.

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:10.560
This week we're in Pembrokeshire with comedian Josh Widdicombe, taking a trip down memory lane to his treasured family holidays.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:25.440
I came on family holidays for almost a decade, from the age of six to 16.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:29.360
I spent two weeks of summer round the beaches around Pembroke town.

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:45.680
I can kind of make anything nostalgic, so this is quite a confronting thing, coming back here, cos I might find out it was rubbish and it's just me pretending in my mind it was good.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:55.240
This is the campsite I used to camp on with my parents.

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.520
St Petrox Camp Site.

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:08.080
I have no idea how we found it in the time before internet, but once we decided we liked it, we'd do it every year, which was kind of our attitude to everything.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.880
When we'd be putting up our tent, we'd listen to music.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:24.520
Obviously, 1995, I vividly remember buying Country House to help Blur beat Oasis.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:26.640
But it wasn't always that cool.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:30.600
I remember the year when we had Donald, Where's Your Troosers?

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:44.640
The great thing about a tent is however wrong you get it, really, you know, it's never going to be a pleasure even if you get it right, so it doesn't really matter if you get it wrong.

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:48.880
It's going to be an uncomfortable night.

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:53.480
I think we can all agree that move was absolutely astonishing use of the wind.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:21:00.520
This is suspicious, isn't it?

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.800
I wonder whether I'll get to sleep in that.

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:11.880
I'm very tired, so that's a bonus.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:23.560
I think I'm going to quit camping while I'm ahead now.

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:31.120
I've done it, I slept all right, I only woke up every two hours, then I had a nice shower and now I've got some Honey Nut Loops.

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:33.360
Life is seven out of ten.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:51.280
When we'd come to Broad Haven Beach, which was our beach of choice, there was this amazing walk along these lily ponds.

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:53.680
It's unbelievably nice, isn't it?

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:55.760
I... I...

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.520
I... I...

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:03.840
The weird thing is the bit I remember most about this is this bridge with the handle on one side.

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:04.520
Perilous.

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:08.240
Surely they can afford two handles.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:12.880
It's the best way to get to any beach.

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:15.840
Obviously, it means once you're on the beach, you're on the beach.

00:22:14.920 --> 00:22:19.560
If you need to go to the toilet, it's a dune or the sea.

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:26.240
It's much bigger than I remember.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:34.200
I mainly remember the wind.

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:43.400
My parents having to buy a windbreak and you'd put it, hammer it into the beach and you'd basically be sheltering as the wind hit you and it's not particularly relaxing.

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:47.920
Roll up your trousers, Brits on holiday.

00:22:50.120 --> 00:22:53.080
Definitely played cricket on the beach, that's my main memory of that.

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:55.760
Oh, he's gone!

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:56.080
Ooh!

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:01.880
Very low bounce.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:06.760
'All the things you imagine you'd do on a British holiday on the beach.'

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:09.160
Oh, that is so cold!

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:10.840
It can't always have been this cold.

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:11.320
I mean, that's...

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:15.080
That's colder than a cold shower.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:16.560
Bracing.

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.160
Oh, it's quite refreshing after a while.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:29.840
Kind of about four or five when the sun's coming down, we'd go from the beach.

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:40.000
At the other end of the lily ponds is a tearoom, actually called, I think, Ye Olde Cafe.

00:23:44.920 --> 00:24:01.560
I remember a big controversy when one year, they replaced clotted cream with squirty cream on their cream teas and I now feel genuinely worried thinking about that as to whether it'll be clotted or squirty if we go back now.

00:24:00.600 --> 00:24:02.040
I'm going to be gutted if it's squirty.

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:02.560
It's just not the same, is it?

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:19.640
I mean, it's classic Cornish clotted cream.

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:22.400
That's exactly what you're looking for, isn't it?

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:28.840
There's a lot of debate over whether you put the jam or the cream on first.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:33.240
Quite high-level debate as well and you've got to go jam first.

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.160
The cream is the best bit.

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:37.640
It's not just a replacement for butter.

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:57.920
When you look back nostalgically on something, you probably imagined it differently or time has changed it in your head, but it was exactly the same, really.

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:10.880
I'm glad it is, cos I don't think it would have needed to be that nice a place, cos I think it was the circumstance that made it nice and the family holiday.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:11.880
But it just happens that...

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:15.240
I mean, that beach is way better than so many beaches.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:21.520
It's such a nice beach.

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:28.640
I'm very lucky, really, to have got to go there.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:30.000
Maybe too many years in a row.

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:31.480
Could have mixed it up a bit, but it's very nice.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:47.320
Earlier, we heard how new laws have been brought in to tackle the problem of thousands of abandoned and neglected horses.

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:51.480
But as Tom's been finding out, these new powers aren't without their problems.

00:25:57.880 --> 00:26:07.360
Illegally grazing on someone else's land or simply abandoned - it's a crisis affecting thousands of horses.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:10.560
Some are a nuisance, many have welfare issues.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:15.480
But new laws have made it easier for councils and welfare organisations to do something about it.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:27.760
They can seize illegally grazed horses and the RSPCA say they are re-homing more than ever, so where are they all ending up?

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:40.600
The lucky ones come to places like this, World Horse Welfare in Norwich, which re-homes neglected and abandoned horses.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:43.400
The little one having fun in here.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:51.320
This lively chap is Huckleberry, one of the first horses seized under the new legislation in England.

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:52.320
Jacko Jackson helped to rescue him.

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:56.600
Some viewers may find his photos distressing.

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:59.800
So tell me the story of Huckleberry before he came here.

00:26:58.840 --> 00:27:01.360
He was found initially in Suffolk.

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:07.600
Lots of reports about him and a friend, another horse, being dumped on land.

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:11.000
By the time we got to them, the friend was dead.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:13.320
And what was that scene like when you arrived?

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:16.440
It was just bones and lots of maggots.

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:22.080
What did you feel when you walked in to the field and saw him and what was his friend now dead on the ground?

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:33.640
Initially, I was glad that we'd found him, but having seen the dead one, um, we were going to move heaven and earth to get him out of there.

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:35.520
He needed to live.

00:27:34.600 --> 00:27:37.040
And live like a proper equine.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:40.720
And now you can see him living life to the full, what do you think about that?

00:27:40.880 --> 00:27:44.160
We did the right thing.

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:45.840
No two ways about that.

00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:49.000
And this Act has enabled it to happen.

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:55.520
But not all horses seized under the new law have happy endings like Huckleberry.

00:27:54.640 --> 00:28:01.440
For others, being rescued is the end of the road and they are humanely destroyed.

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:14.160
And that might come as a shock to some, to hear that if you complain about a fly-grazed or neglected horse, it could end up being rounded up, but then put down.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:22.400
As Britain's main animal welfare charity, the RSPCA lobbied for this tough new legislation, so how do they feel about it now?

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:35.480
The new law was necessary because we had 3,000-3,500 horses being illegally kept on other people's land and the RSPCA and other horse welfare organisations were having to pick up the pieces.

00:28:34.600 --> 00:28:40.080
So now you've got the law, you think there are roughly 3,500 or so horses out there that could be helped by this?

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:44.000
The good news is the law has been in place for six months now.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:46.920
Many local authorities are already using it.

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:56.760
We reckon the number of horses that have already been removed is probably in the hundreds, so actually it's a really good example of a piece of law that's working.

00:28:55.800 --> 00:29:00.760
Do you have any idea roughly what proportion end up being euthanised?

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.800
I think it would be round about half would be euthanised.

00:29:02.160 --> 00:29:07.240
And how happy do you think the public are with that fact?

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:15.960
Many of these horses are suffering anyway, not just cruelly treated, but they're in problem places, they could be next to a road or railway line - that's a real danger.

00:29:22.520 --> 00:29:34.840
So, if you can accept that half of all seized horses are put down, then you might think that the end of the horse crisis is in sight.

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:37.360
But you might have to think again.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:40.480
That's because there's a catch and it all comes down to money.

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:50.760
Back on the road, council animal welfare officer Steve Gale is with horse owner Ian, who we met earlier.

00:29:51.240 --> 00:29:53.680
Have you got some swivels on your chain?

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:54.600
Yeah.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:54.920
Yeah?

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:56.440
What are the swivels...?

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:57.360
To stop the chain knotting up.

00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:01.960
What can happen, with the horse going round and round, it can get a knot in the chain.

00:30:01.000 --> 00:30:03.280
Is that the only one you've got?

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:05.080
You should have two.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:11.840
Steve's handing out advice, not sanctions, and there's a good reason for that.

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:16.680
So you've got the new law and the power to take them away, but does that mean the problem is solved?

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:19.160
I wish it was as simple as that, Tom.

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:24.840
The problem is, it's the cost of actually upholding the law and trying to enforce it.

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:34.480
The average ballpark figure with an equine bill is 1,000 or £1,500 per horse to take it away and look after it for four days.

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:40.400
Which obviously, if you've got a huge problem within your local authority area, it can be quite burdensome on that local authority.

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:41.200
Mm-hm.

00:30:40.280 --> 00:30:45.760
And local authorities are enduring a lot of cuts and more in the pipeline.

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:46.120
Yes.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.040
What are your worries there?

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:52.280
That some local authorities won't see it as a priority and something they don't have to do.

00:30:51.880 --> 00:31:00.400
I would urge local authorities to try and keep on board with this, because if we relax a little bit, probably a huger cost in the future, when the situation is out of control.

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:14.400
With bailiff fees of well over £1,000 a time, it's likely many fly-grazing horses won't be a priority for removal unless there is evidence of serious welfare problems.

00:31:14.080 --> 00:31:22.800
With threats over future funding to enforce the new law, there's a danger this apparently effective measure could be undermined.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:28.200
The battle against illegal grazing and neglect is far from over.

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:33.680
We'd like to know whether abandoned or fly-grazed horses are a problem in your area.

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:37.400
And if you think they should be taken away.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:39.440
Let us know via our website or join the conversation on Twitter.

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:55.040
I'm in glorious Gloucestershire, at Batsford Arboretum, a sanctuary for tree species from all over the world.

00:31:54.080 --> 00:32:00.960
Earlier on, we scanned this ancient tree and discovered it was too rotten inside to be structurally stable.

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:06.440
Well, now it is time to watch this tree come down, so, Matthew, what's the plan?

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:15.000
Well, we'll get the cherry picker in, take it down in small sections to a height where we can fell it in one.

00:32:14.560 --> 00:32:15.680
Obviously, there's been quite a bit of work happening already.

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:17.120
Yep.

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:21.160
We had it all skimmed up, so we'll just step back and see how they get on.

00:32:20.200 --> 00:32:21.360
We'll let them get going.

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:58.800
Even though it's been cut down, this beech will live on, by helping other trees to thrive.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:01.040
The waste wood is chipped... and used to protect other newly-planted saplings.

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:07.280
All part of the cycle of life, death and rebirth at Batsford.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:12.880
Ah, it's a brilliant bit of kit, this!

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:16.600
Just watch it disappear.

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:21.720
Look at the size of this one, here we go!

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:29.960
Unbelievable.

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:32.200
Best piece of kit you could ever have.

00:33:32.920 --> 00:33:37.600
Well, this deadwood is not the end of the story.

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:48.720
Later on, I'll be planting some new species that will be taking root in 2016, and talking of looking ahead to the rest of the year, if you haven't got your Countryfile calendar, sold in aid of Children In Need, yet, just go to the website...

00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:49.360
For all of the details.

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:55.080
Was I shouting then?

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:55.560
Think I might have been!

00:33:59.680 --> 00:34:04.920
The BBC's Food And Farming Awards for 2016 are underway.

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:10.400
As part of it, Countryfile is looking for its next Farming Hero.

00:34:09.880 --> 00:34:13.159
Someone who you think embodies the best of British farming.

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:14.840
Here's Adam with more.

00:34:14.679 --> 00:34:20.000
Last year's search threw up hundreds of great nominations.

00:34:19.080 --> 00:34:20.400
In the end, it came down to just three.

00:34:21.199 --> 00:34:24.920
Cameron Hendry was one of them.

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:31.080
At just 17, he found himself running the family farm after his dad's untimely death.

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:41.199
We were all incredibly impressed with the work that Cameron and his family are doing in what is extremely challenging farming countryside.

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:44.719
We've arranged some special extra farming help, Cameron.

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:51.320
They'll come and help you on the farm over this next year and help you get through this difficult coming year ahead.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.120
Cameron was certainly a worthy finalist.

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:21.640
And he won the hearts of many through his determination and bravery and me and the other judges were moved by the way he took on a 2,500-acre family hill farm after the sudden death of his father on Christmas Day in 2014.

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:33.880
A year down the line, I've travelled back up to Cameron's farm in Perthshire to see how he's getting on.

00:35:33.200 --> 00:35:42.840
Cameron might have taken on the lion's share of the work, but it's his mum, Marianne, that's been holding the family together.

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:45.440
I'm meeting her first, to find out how they've all been coping.

00:35:46.720 --> 00:35:48.680
Hello, how are you?

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:49.240
Good to see you again!

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:51.160
And you.

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:52.200
So, how are things?

00:35:51.480 --> 00:35:55.000
It's been, what, nearly a year now since your husband passed away.

00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:55.640
Yes.

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:59.680
Was 2015 tough?

00:35:58.840 --> 00:36:02.280
Very, absolutely.

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:05.560
There's no easy way to describe it, it's been our worst year, I have to say.

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:14.760
Apart from all the hard work, all the actual physical work, having to do it without a husband and a dad has been really difficult.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:19.040
I was very impressed with Cameron when I met him a year ago.

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:20.880
How has he been getting on?

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:21.640
Great.

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:22.200
Yeah, he's amazing.

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:24.400
He's an amazing person.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:29.160
He's extremely driven, he's got the temperament of his dad.

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:32.920
Grumpy at times, moody like any normal teenager!

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:35.200
But that also got him through.

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:43.720
Works very hard and apart from all the hard work, obviously then having to do it without your dad is a different matter altogether.

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:46.040
So he just hasn't had his dad to share it with.

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:46.840
No.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:59.160
There's nothing like your dad to actually have a good old man-to-man with, and ask him for advice, and that's gone.

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:07.240
And you, as a mum...

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:09.480
I suppose you're having to be a bit of a rock and hold the fort.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:19.200
Yeah, I've had to turn into Mum and Dad at the same time and I'm an OK mum, I think, but I'm a rubbish dad, I've decided.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:19.600
Um, but we're working on it.

00:37:18.760 --> 00:37:22.600
It's difficult.

00:37:21.880 --> 00:37:29.920
He's almost taken over the role of the dad at times in the house.

00:37:28.960 --> 00:37:31.480
Which is upsetting for a mum to see.

00:37:30.520 --> 00:37:39.320
But at the same time, it's endearing as well, it's quite lovely to see how we've all pulled together as a family.

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:43.840
Without Marianne's support, Cameron would have undoubtedly struggled.

00:37:43.200 --> 00:37:50.080
But the promised help has been arriving.

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:56.200
Agricultural consultant Kevin Stewart has been helping them plan for the future.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:57.880
Adam, this is Kevin.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:37:58.760
Hi, Kevin, good to see you.

00:38:00.960 --> 00:38:04.520
I hear it's been a pretty busy time on the farm?

00:38:03.680 --> 00:38:06.480
How are you getting on with the consultancy?

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:07.560
What are the plans?

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:17.600
The great thing about Cameron is he keeps coming up with opportunities, so what my job effectively is is to be that sounding board.

00:38:17.080 --> 00:38:22.920
You've got your head down, haven't you, working with the sheep and the cows.

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:28.120
Quite difficult sometimes to come up with a business plan and look at the books carefully.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:28.800
Definitely.

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:36.400
It's great to have another pair of eyes there to see what other opportunities there are out there for us.

00:38:53.680 --> 00:38:59.040
When I was here last, Cameron, it was a beautiful, almost spring day.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:38:59.840
It's a bit different now!

00:38:59.080 --> 00:39:05.000
No, it's still a good day round here, but it's a wee bit rougher since the last time you were here.

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:08.800
And it looks like a pretty hard farm to work.

00:39:07.920 --> 00:39:09.160
Yes, it is a hard farm to work.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:13.120
We just have to cope with the weather conditions and just keep going with it.

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:16.080
And how are you feeling a year on?

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:20.480
It's been really tough, but we're just staying positive and carrying on.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:23.360
Just take every day as it comes.

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:28.800
What you've gone through, Cameron, to still be so positive about it, you're still a real farming hero for me, so well done.

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:40.920
Like a true farming hero, Cameron is not resting on his laurels.

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:44.680
He's already slimmed down his beef herd from 800 to around 80 cattle.

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:49.520
It's quality, not quantity that matters.

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:53.480
So now he's looking to establish a herd of pedigree Luing cattle.

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:58.080
Charles Symons and Ted Fox are from the Luing Cattle Association.

00:39:57.400 --> 00:40:01.000
Will Cameron's cows make the pedigree grade?

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:06.920
What are you looking for to assess these, then?

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:09.920
We've already gone through the provenance.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:13.320
We're now assessing them to make sure they're Luing type.

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:20.000
So really, the advantage of having registered animals means you can sell for beef, but also in the pedigree world.

00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:20.280
Absolutely.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:20.480
You're looking to sell pedigree stock.

00:40:19.560 --> 00:40:29.240
It might take Cameron a wee while to get worked into that, but he should hopefully be able to establish himself as a pedigree breeder and hence sell for more money, which is what we're all trying to do.

00:40:29.440 --> 00:40:32.920
So what should a good Luing look like?

00:40:32.200 --> 00:40:34.400
They're working on poor forage through the winter months.

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:41.200
They want a big, broad muzzle, a big head, they want to be deep in the chest cavity, but temperament is everything.

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:45.040
Especially with a young lad like this, working with them on his own.

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:47.960
Temperament is everything and keeping them easy to handle.

00:40:47.360 --> 00:40:50.400
And the chances of getting the herd registered?

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:55.520
I think animals like this will definitely qualify.

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:56.240
We think these are excellent examples of the breed.

00:40:55.400 --> 00:40:58.840
Cameron, are you quite excited about going for the pedigree status?

00:40:57.920 --> 00:41:00.160
My dad never had that sort of chance.

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:08.360
He was always fattening them, so I'm taking the farm in a completely new direction and changing it up.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:15.560
In a few years' time, you'll probably have one of the best Luing herds in the country.

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:29.240
With the help Cameron has been receiving and not least his own hard work and determination, some real progress has been made.

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:33.720
It might have been a tough year, but there's light at the end of the tunnel.

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:40.160
I've left Cameron to get on with the never-ending daily jobs on the farm.

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:43.840
He's certainly an inspiration and deserved to be in the final three last year.

00:41:43.080 --> 00:41:49.120
Now, it's over to you to help find the Countryfile Farming Hero for 2016.

00:41:50.560 --> 00:41:59.120
This award is for a farmer or farming family who have made a difference through their heroic actions.

00:41:58.960 --> 00:42:05.280
The judges want to hear about farmers who have come to the rescue of others, man or beast, at a time of need.

00:42:04.720 --> 00:42:15.640
They could've organised emergency animal housing for their fellow farmer, have helped their neighbour when times were bleak or given city kids their only experience of agricultural life.

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:19.840
We'll celebrate the achievements of truly remarkable people who make our countryside a better place.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:29.600
Our winner will be someone who has gone above and beyond to help their farming friends and neighbours and of whom we can all be proud.

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:34.840
So if you know someone who fits that bill, we'd love to hear from you.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:39.480
Visit our website and tell us why they deserve to be our next farming hero.

00:42:38.680 --> 00:42:41.600
But be quick, because entries close in a week.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:50.000
..So names sent in after that won't be considered.

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:54.800
Remember, if you're watching on demand, nominations may have already closed.

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:56.880
Details, including terms and conditions, are on our website.

00:43:03.600 --> 00:43:09.720
I'm in picturesque Gloucestershire, a county of rolling hills and golden villages.

00:43:09.520 --> 00:43:18.600
With the Forest of Dean and Slimbridge Wetland Centre, it's a hotspot for overwintering and native birds.

00:43:18.040 --> 00:43:22.440
But the county is also home to some of the world's most magnificent raptors.

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:31.360
I'm at the International Centre for Birds Of Prey, where ground-breaking work is being done to help protect these remarkable masters of flight.

00:43:34.160 --> 00:43:42.480
The centre was founded in 1967 by world-famous falconer Phillip Glasier.

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:47.280
He was passionate about birds of prey and wanted to teach others about them and their value in the world.

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:59.720
Phillip's daughter Jemima Parry-Jones has continued in her father's footsteps, devoting her life to falconry and raptor conservation.

00:43:58.960 --> 00:44:06.160
Today, the centre holds one of the largest collections of birds of prey in the world and has recently opened a new raptor hospital.

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:07.680
How many birds do you have here?

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:09.480
About 240 at the moment.

00:44:08.600 --> 00:44:11.720
How many different species?

00:44:10.880 --> 00:44:14.400
I think we're at about 74 species at the moment.

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:19.360
Do people tend to bring you injured birds of prey here?

00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:23.880
Yes, they do, and it's really important that a place like this will accept them.

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:27.080
We get calls day and night about all sorts of things.

00:44:26.240 --> 00:44:32.040
My absolute favourite, a lady phoned me up and she said, I found a baby bird, and I said, "Fine, what do you think it is?"

00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:35.160
She said, "I think it's a dodo".

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:36.840
The very last one!

00:44:35.920 --> 00:44:38.600
It was a pigeon!

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:39.280
That's disappointing!

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:48.080
And the aim, is it to rehabilitate them and get them back into the wild, or do you tend to keep them once they've been injured?

00:44:47.160 --> 00:44:50.800
No, it's not rehabilitation if you keep hanging on to them, so the aim is absolutely to get them back into the wild.

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:58.480
Every year, the centre takes in up to 100 injured birds of prey.

00:44:57.640 --> 00:45:01.400
Curator Holly Cale has been rehabilitating a peregrine falcon.

00:45:00.520 --> 00:45:06.040
It was hit by a plane and had to have its entire wing rebuilt.

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:09.200
You can see here there's a patch of feathers there...

00:45:09.400 --> 00:45:11.720
A slightly different colour.

00:45:11.440 --> 00:45:20.160
Those are his adult feathers that have grown through where they had to pluck the area to do the operation.

00:45:19.880 --> 00:45:26.520
We also fixed some of his feathers, so you can see he's got a full set of feathers down there that had ended up broken as well.

00:45:25.800 --> 00:45:27.720
So, like feather implants?

00:45:27.200 --> 00:45:30.240
Yes, it's called imping.

00:45:29.320 --> 00:45:41.400
We can take a feather that's been moulted by another bird and we insert it with a little bit of bamboo and some glue into his own feather stump where it had been broken.

00:45:40.640 --> 00:45:41.800
That's remarkable.

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:45.840
So we fixed those and we fixed the wing and he's now in training, gaining fitness and physiotherapy, if you like, to get him back to the wild.

00:45:46.120 --> 00:45:47.440
Wow.

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:48.720
Well, I'll stand back while you do this exercising and see what's involved.

00:45:59.360 --> 00:46:04.160
It's astonishing that wing even works.

00:46:08.600 --> 00:46:20.720
It is so wonderful to witness a native bird of prey like this being given a second chance, flying again and soon to be released back into the wild, but it's not just native birds that the centre is helping.

00:46:25.640 --> 00:46:30.080
Vultures are one of the world's most spectacular and most endangered birds of prey.

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:39.440
The centre breeds several species, including the Andean condor, a New World vulture.

00:46:38.520 --> 00:46:39.800
Condor coming!

00:46:38.840 --> 00:46:40.160
Coming, Condor!

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:41.040
You want to come up here?

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:41.320
Hello!

00:46:44.680 --> 00:46:46.600
Wow, who's this?

00:46:45.840 --> 00:46:49.720
This is Marcus.

00:46:49.320 --> 00:46:52.440
Marcus is a baby Andean Condor.

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:58.160
They do seem to have this quite unfortunate reputation as they're associated with death.

00:46:57.200 --> 00:47:05.360
Yes, it's a real shame, because they're doing incredibly badly and they're incredibly clean animals and they're incredibly important in terms of clearing up.

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:15.440
They reckon that of all the wildlife on the Serengeti, the vultures clear up more dead animals than all the carnivores put together, so they are really important.

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:19.000
Like all children, she wants to put her head inside everything.

00:47:18.240 --> 00:47:19.520
Look at that wingspan!

00:47:18.760 --> 00:47:20.080
I know, it's huge, isn't it?

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:30.280
Vultures in south Asia were almost totally wiped out in the 1990s.

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:36.080
Numbers collapsed by 97% and scientists struggled to work out why.

00:47:37.360 --> 00:47:43.720
What was the reason for the decline, for that really quick and dramatic decline?

00:47:43.200 --> 00:48:04.680
It turned out to be a drug called diclofenac, which is a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory, that was given to cattle across south Asia and because vultures will all congregate at one carcass, it only takes a few cattle to be treated, it was less than 2% of the cattle, to wipe out over 40 million birds.

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:13.920
Jemima has spent the last 15 years helping bring the surviving populations in India and Nepal back from the brink.

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:29.200
We started to design the breeding aviary, facilities for incubation, brooding and in fact, this year, I'm proud to say that we bred over 60 young, which is really wonderful for a place like India, which had never done this sort of thing before.

00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:32.800
Next year, hopefully, the first release will start.

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:42.200
But the scale of the numbers that had dropped from 40 million or so right down to almost single-digit thousands, how long will it take to replenish that loss?

00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:47.120
Oh, that's going to take a long time, but nature is such an amazing thing, really.

00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:57.040
And although vultures are quite slow breeders, once they start getting going, so long as there's no drug out there that will kill them, certainly reasonable numbers, I hope in my lifetime, anyway.

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:08.000
The future for South Asia's vultures is looking up.

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:15.360
But another species, the hooded vulture from Africa, has just been relisted as critically endangered, so there's still plenty to do.

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:35.400
It's fantastic to think that the work being done here in Gloucestershire and the skills and expertise of Jemima are being shared with conservationists across the world to help protect the future of birds of prey and fabulous vultures like these.

00:49:53.720 --> 00:49:58.320
I'm in Gloucestershire, and whilst Ellie's been on a flight of fancy...

00:49:57.560 --> 00:50:00.080
Oh!

00:50:00.080 --> 00:50:09.880
..I'm rooted to the ground at Batsford Arboretum, home to a wide variety of unusual tree species from around the world.

00:50:09.440 --> 00:50:17.280
The oriental plants and water feature here were the brainchild of Victorian diplomat Lord Redesdale.

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:22.120
But Redesdale's love of the Orient did not stop with the landscaping.

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:41.120
Lord Redesdale converted to Buddhism and hidden amongst the trees are Buddhist-themed bronzes, a Japanese bridge and a peace pavilion that reflected his love of the culture.

00:50:40.560 --> 00:50:47.200
Today, it's not unusual to find local t'ai chi groups practising their art among the trees here.

00:50:46.720 --> 00:51:01.400
Earlier on, I witnessed the sad demise of one of the Arboretum's oldest trees, but just like Buddha here, keeping a silent watch over the grounds, Batsford's tale is also one of death and rebirth.

00:51:01.680 --> 00:51:09.960
The Arboretum is part of a programme that hosts rare trees, ones endangered in their native environments.

00:51:09.120 --> 00:51:15.400
Here, they are safeguarded and preserved for the future.

00:51:14.880 --> 00:51:18.360
It's something which makes head gardener Matthew especially proud.

00:51:17.680 --> 00:51:21.160
Right, Matthew, these young arrivals are incredibly precious, aren't they?

00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:22.440
They are.

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:26.880
That's Picea omorika, which is Serbian spruce.

00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:29.600
Like a classic Christmas tree.

00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:44.840
You know, in its wild state, it's becoming endangered, whether it's through deforestation, logging, other environmental factors, but the wild form is really quite unusual.

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:53.200
So this is actually as it should be - tight, compact, so the snow can fall off it.

00:51:52.480 --> 00:51:57.240
As they get bred and you go down the line of seed, they lose that originality.

00:51:56.520 --> 00:52:00.960
And so the idea then with this project is to try and keep that gene pool?

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:12.240
Keep that gene pool, and what will happen, these will go in safe sites in the Arboretum and if ever any of these go extinct in the wild, there'll be some plants here which can be re-propagated and maybe even put back into the wild.

00:52:12.480 --> 00:52:16.440
Matthew thinks he's got the perfect spot to make these foreign firs feel right at home.

00:52:48.360 --> 00:52:50.280
No prizes for guessing where it's going!

00:52:51.200 --> 00:52:52.480
Actually, which one do you want to put in, though?

00:52:51.840 --> 00:52:53.320
That's the question.

00:52:52.440 --> 00:52:53.960
Let's go for the big one.

00:52:53.000 --> 00:52:54.360
Let's unwrap that.

00:53:03.400 --> 00:53:07.560
How long would you expect a tree like this to live for?

00:53:06.920 --> 00:53:10.160
It could be here for the next hundred years or more.

00:53:09.640 --> 00:53:15.040
I tell you what, it's got a nice view to spend the next hundred years.

00:53:14.320 --> 00:53:15.440
What a nice place to live!

00:53:15.560 --> 00:53:18.560
Just a bit of frost protection, is it?

00:53:17.600 --> 00:53:19.200
Um, no.

00:53:18.320 --> 00:53:24.480
This will just keep the grass down, we'll get the guard round it, stop any deer grazing on it.

00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:26.320
Do you have a problem with deer?

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:28.200
Yeah, we have a little bit.

00:53:27.640 --> 00:53:32.160
They'll always go for that one plant that you don't want it to go for.

00:53:32.080 --> 00:53:35.680
Do they like the foreign stuff?

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:36.720
They're not fussy.

00:53:35.920 --> 00:53:37.840
If it's foreign, it's probably better.

00:53:39.080 --> 00:53:40.720
Right, are you happy with that?

00:53:39.840 --> 00:53:41.400
I'm happy.

00:53:46.520 --> 00:53:54.040
Hopefully, Gloucestershire will become a home from home and these precious specimens will grow into great giants.

00:53:55.360 --> 00:53:58.720
Lord Redesdale would be proud.

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:09.880
So, from the death of a mighty tree to the new life of a small one, it's all part of the great cycle of rebirth here at Batsford.

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:12.480
Kind of makes you feel at peace with the universe.

00:54:44.080 --> 00:54:48.200
Well, that's all we've got time for from the tranquillity of Batsford Arboretum.

00:54:48.560 --> 00:54:55.840
Next week, I'll be exploring the picturesque fishing village of Clovelly as winter takes hold.

00:54:56.840 --> 00:54:59.480
Hope you can join us then.

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:03.040
Let your New Year start with a bang and visit an explosive new China.

00:55:02.080 --> 00:55:03.600
Look what China has achieved.

00:55:59.240 --> 00:56:00.320
Celebrate a country 4,000 years in the making.

00:56:01.320 --> 00:56:02.440
China begins here.

00:56:01.680 --> 00:56:02.920
Getting out into the streets and seeing what it's all about.

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:03.400
And see this little-known nation in a brand-new light.

00:56:08.840 --> 00:56:10.120
China Season...

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:13.480
So distinctive and so brilliant.

00:56:12.840 --> 00:56:13.960
..Starts with...

00:56:13.000 --> 00:56:15.560
Not so long ago, you just beat a rug for a good time.

00:56:18.120 --> 00:56:20.560
After this, we can do the other carpet in the lounge.

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:21.160
Since then, things have perked up.

00:56:20.200 --> 00:56:21.360
Oh, my God!

00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:21.760
We're going on holiday.

00:56:20.840 --> 00:56:22.600
Are you ready?

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:25.720
Yeah!

00:56:24.920 --> 00:56:26.040
See one family live through a spare time revolution.

00:56:25.240 --> 00:56:26.520
You've turned into Wham!

00:56:25.600 --> 00:56:27.880
From no time to me time, let's go Back In Time For The Weekend.
